1
00:06:57,041 --> 00:06:58,835
So, our...(we) have Eid.

2
00:06:59,127 --> 00:06:59,836
(We) celebrate Eid.

3
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:02,046
We celebrate Eid-e-Milad.

4
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:05,341
And...there is a wedding.

5
00:07:06,843 --> 00:07:08,928
Wedding is our biggest festival.

6
00:07:09,178 --> 00:07:10,263
It's a unique practice.

7
00:07:10,721 --> 00:07:12,390
(We) get married in our way.

8
00:07:12,390 --> 00:07:13,349
The <i>jaani</i> (groomsmen) go.

9
00:07:14,475 --> 00:07:16,561
The groom's side first treat with food.

10
00:07:16,561 --> 00:07:17,979
After treating with food, <i>mehndi</i> (henna) is worn.

11
00:07:17,979 --> 00:07:19,689
The day after the <i>mehndi</i>, the groom is prepared.

12
00:07:19,689 --> 00:07:20,606
After preparing the groom...

13
00:07:21,399 --> 00:07:22,233
..(groomsmen) go to the bride's.

14
00:07:22,233 --> 00:07:23,192
After going there, the wedding is done.

15
00:07:23,192 --> 00:07:24,610
After finishing the wedding,

16
00:07:24,944 --> 00:07:25,903
many girls come.

17
00:07:27,029 --> 00:07:28,072
The bride stays for a night.

18
00:07:28,239 --> 00:07:29,657
Money is gifted to see the girl (bride)'s face.

19
00:07:30,241 --> 00:07:31,659
Folks return after gifting money.

20
00:07:32,243 --> 00:07:34,453
Three–four months later (they) invite and bring back the girl.

21
00:07:34,996 --> 00:07:36,289
Like in other societies,

22
00:07:37,456 --> 00:07:39,041
immediately after getting married,

23
00:07:39,333 --> 00:07:40,459
the husband has the wife there (his place),

24
00:07:40,877 --> 00:07:41,586
it doesn't happen like this with Gujjars.

25
00:07:42,128 --> 00:07:43,171
In Gujjars, the wedding is over,

26
00:07:44,046 --> 00:07:45,882
the girls who come with <i>makaḷābō</i>

27
00:07:45,882 --> 00:07:47,216
the bride goes back (to her parents) with them.

28
00:07:47,675 --> 00:07:49,802
Two–three months later, she returns...

29
00:07:50,136 --> 00:07:50,803
they bring her back.

30
00:07:51,470 --> 00:07:53,014
When they bring her back after two-three months,

31
00:07:53,014 --> 00:07:54,682
the girl (bride) comes with them.

32
00:07:55,141 --> 00:07:58,394
they (girls with bride) are gifted (salwar-) suits and money when they return.

33
00:07:59,187 --> 00:08:01,314
This is a type of custom.

34
00:08:01,397 --> 00:08:02,773
During Gujjar weddings,

35
00:08:02,773 --> 00:08:04,233
first of all, <i>mehndi</i> was applied.

36
00:08:05,735 --> 00:08:06,694
That day was called <i>maiḷ</i>.

37
00:08:06,694 --> 00:08:08,154
The day after applying henna,

38
00:08:09,030 --> 00:08:10,406
The groom is adorned with what is called a <i>janyār</i>.

39
00:08:10,489 --> 00:08:12,241
He used to have a turban and planks on his head.

40
00:08:12,617 --> 00:08:13,326
Over the turban,

41
00:08:13,618 --> 00:08:14,243
[groom] used to tie a <i>sēh'rā</i>.

42
00:08:14,952 --> 00:08:16,746
After tying the <i>sēh'rā</i>, they would put on a <i>lāccā</i> from this side...

43
00:08:17,496 --> 00:08:18,456
...red in colour.

44
00:08:19,248 --> 00:08:20,541
<i>Lācā</i> is worn over the vest.

45
00:08:21,375 --> 00:08:23,669
They put <i>lācā</i>, and, over it, a money-garland on him.

46
00:08:24,253 --> 00:08:24,795
After putting the garland

47
00:08:24,795 --> 00:08:26,756
everyone would recite the <i>bait</i> (song) and sing songs.

48
00:08:28,174 --> 00:08:29,800
That thing is also slowly vanishing...I mean...

49
00:08:30,301 --> 00:08:31,969
Now we are bringing <i>lācā</i> also which, I mean, one

50
00:08:31,969 --> 00:08:33,596
that goes on in a common world...

51
00:08:33,596 --> 00:08:34,305
(We've) finished it.

52
00:08:34,305 --> 00:08:35,640
We even changed our <i>sēhrā</i>.

53
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:39,101
sewing <i>tehmet kurta</i> for wedding is stopped.

54
00:08:39,519 --> 00:08:40,686
(We) wear <i>sherwani</i>.

55
00:08:41,270 --> 00:08:44,649
So...I think we should maintain our same culture.
